INT. BELL TOWER - SNIPER NEST - DAWN

A stone shaft, open to the weather. Cracked bell hangs silent overhead, green with age. Pigeon droppings crust the floor. Through the louvres, a ruined village burns in slow grey ribbons.

SGT. MOSES TEMBO, 40s, huge, patient, lies flat behind a rifle. Scar across his eyebrow. Every breath measured.

PVT. FERGAL ROURKE, 19, freckled, trembling, crouches at the rim of the stairwell with a field phone.

CAPT. ANTON VASQUEZ, 30s, in a dented helmet, studies a map pinned to the wall with a bayonet.

ROURKE
Nothing on the line.

TEMBO
Keep trying.

VASQUEZ
They've cut it.

ROURKE
Then who do I call?

VASQUEZ
Nobody.

ROURKE
(overlapping)
We're stuck up here!

TEMBO
(overlapping)
Shut your mouth, boy.

VASQUEZ
(overlapping, to both)
Quiet. Both of you.

Silence. Pigeons shift on the beams. Tembo's eye stays glued to the scope.

TEMBO
Movement. Left of the well.

Vasquez drops beside him. He raises binoculars. He holds them very still.

VASQUEZ
How many?

TEMBO
Three. Maybe four.

Rourke drops the phone. It clatters. All three freeze.

ROURKE
(whispering)
Sorry.

VASQUEZ
Pick it up. Slowly.

He does. Below, a boot scrapes stone. Closer than any of them wants.

TEMBO
They're in the church.

VASQUEZ
Then they're under us.

Tembo shifts the rifle. He exhales. The great bell shivers, ever so slightly, as if remembering.

ROURKE
Sarge--

TEMBO
Not now.

Wood creaks on the stair. Rourke raises his rifle with both hands. The barrel wavers. Vasquez rests a hand on his shoulder.

VASQUEZ
Easy, son.

Light from the stairwell. Then a shape in the light.

Tembo fires.
